let's talk about what is known in Latin  as post hoc ergo propter hoc this  translates in english as after this  therefore because of this this is also  known simply as the post hoc fallacy  let's say this I live on a farm and I  have a rooster every morning right  before sunrise the rooster crows can I  conclude from this that the rooster  causes the Sun to come up every day  that's a famous example that's used to  illustrate the post hoc fallacy we have  a post hoc fallacy when an argument  assumes that just because one event  occurred after another that there must  be a causal link between these two  events just because event B followed  event a then event B must have been  caused by event a so to break down the  fallacy the premise is a occurred before  B the conclusion is therefore a caused B  if there is an implicit premise that  assumes when a occurs before B then a  causes B because and only because a  occurs before B then we have a post hoc  fallacy the argument is invalid and this  is what's known as a causal fallacy or a  questionable cause fallacy because a  cause is being misidentified in order to  make this argument valid we would need  to take that implicit premise and  replace it with actual reasons why a  causes B a causes B because X or because  Y or because Z then it might be safe to  say that a causes B obviously a given  event can be the cause of another event  that closely follows but not merely  because it occurred first a further case  needs to be made in order to make that  link but the post hoc fallacy can be  especially tempting to make because as  humans our perceptions often fall back  on instinct if we're not careful events  in close succession  can easily appear to suggest causality  just take all of the parents who believe  that vaccines caused their child's  autism essentially only because the  autism symptoms appeared after the child  was vaccinated the human mind is always  looking to connect dots when it comes to  sequences and causality the child was  doing just fine before and soon after  the vaccines the child started showing  signs of autism someone arguing a post  hoc fallacy is often failing to  entertain the possibility of a  coincidence or that the second event or  circumstance may have caused the first  event or that there are other unknown  factors relating to the two events this  miniseries is made possible in part by  brilliant if you want to become an even  better critical thinker you should check  out this online learning service  brilliant is a site where you can learn  about logic fallacies multi-level  thinking and countless other topics  related to philosophy mathematics and  science  what sets brilliant apart is that you're  actually learning by working hands-on  with real-life problems one of their  principles is effective learning is  active and this makes a lot of sense  there's a ton you can learn from passive  activities reading books watching online  videos but you're never really taking  your understanding to the next level and  applying what you're learning until  you're able to work with real-world  scenarios and that's what brilliant  allows you to do master anything from  probability to physics to the concepts  of artificial intelligence and  everything in between  sign up for free by going to brilliant  org slash critical thinking I've put a  link in the description under this video  and the first 200 people who visit that  URL will get 20% off an annual  membership visit brilliant org slash  critical thinking
 

